f functions defined?","options":["It is always defined by multiplying all functions together.","It is defined only at a single point of $X$.","It ignores the behaviour of finite partial sums.","It is the limit of its partial sums, when they converge."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0976094404e2038bb36963dd","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For Riemann-integrable terms on $X=[a,b]$ with finite $a<b$, which convergence hypothesis is used by the stated integration theorem?","options":["The series $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n$ converges pointwise on $X$.","The terms $f_n$ themselves converge uniformly to $0$ on $X$.","The series $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n'$ of derivatives converges uniformly on $X$.","The series $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n$ converges uniformly on $X$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e6d672e599de953e62ce6e40","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X=[a,b]$ with finite $a<b$. Suppose $f_n$ are Riemann integrable and $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n$ converges uniformly on $X$. Which term-by-term integration formula is valid?","options":["$\\int_X \\ \\left(\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n(x)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty}\\int_X f_n(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_X \\ \\left(\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n(x)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\prod_{n = 1}^{\\infty}\\int_X f_n(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_X f_1(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n(x)$","$\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty}\\int_X f_n(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x = 0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c91953815d43ffdba875052","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Assume the stated differentiability result applies. If $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n \\to f$ and $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n' \\rightrightarrows g$ on $(a,b)$, what is $f'$ on $(a,b)$?","options":["$\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n$","$f_1'$","$0$","$g$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4a4ad04469858d18c663460a","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is it not enough to look only at the individual functions $f_n$ when defining $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n$?","options":["Because convergence is a statement about the sequence of partial sums $S_N$.","Because each $f_n$ must be integrated first, and the series is the sum of those integrals.","Because the individual $f_n$ need not be defined at every point of $X$.","Because convergence requires the partial sums to be increasing in $N$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"22a13027964a3b3d6fb8ca61","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"On a finite closed interval $X=[a,b]$ with $a<b$, a student invokes the stated uniform-convergence theorem to integrate Riemann-integrable terms. Which hypothesis must they check?","options":["The existence of the very first partial sum $S_1$ only.","Uniform convergence of $\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n$ on $X$.","The fact that $X$ has at least one point.","The notation $f_n(x)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5b0be99154acdb7780588422","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $S_N(x) = \\sum_{n = 1}^{N} f_n(x)$ and suppose $S_N$ converges uniformly to $f$ on $X$. Which conclusion matches the definition?","options":["$f_N$ converges uniformly to $f$ on $X$ by definition of the series.","$\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n$ converges uniformly to $f$ on $X$.","$S_N(x) = f_n(x)$ for every $n$ and $N$.","$\\sum_{n = 1}^{\\infty} f_n$ cannot be assigned a function limit."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1f9b0a7c16cf4c21ce6fe1eb","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How is an infinite series of functions defined through partial sums?","options":["By studying the limit of the individual terms $f_N(x)$ as $N\\to\\infty$.","By studying the limit of $\\prod_{n=1}^N f_n(x)$ as $N\\to\\infty$.","By studying the limit of $S_N(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^N f_n(x)$ as $N\\to\\infty$.","By studying the limit of the averages $\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{n=1}^N f_n(x)$ as $N\\to\\infty$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6be00be1af5203c44e043ee5","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f_n(x)=n x$, what is $S_3(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^3 f_n(x)$?","options":["$6x$","$3x$","$x^3$","$n x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ea578b87b8d0eb3a2f4610a7","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f_n(x)=\\frac{1}{2^n}$, what is $S_2(x)$?","options":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$1$","$\\frac{3}{4}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"36e045eab8bde9ed4edc5e39","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_infinite_series_of_functions_0110c087_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"The series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{n!}$ has which convergence property on $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["It converges pointwise on $\\mathbb{R}$ and uniformly on every compact interval.","It diverges everywhere on $\\mathbb{R}$.","It converges only at $x=0$.","It is not a series of functions on $\\mathbb{R}$ because its individual terms contain different powers of the variable $x$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3ecd7ba8ddb383e8b49d80e1","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}